{"tool_name":"mirsynergy","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"mirsynergy","home":"http://bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/html/Mirsynergy.html","license":"GPL-2.0","summary":"Detect synergistic miRNA regulatory modules by overlapping neighbourhood expansion.","addition_date":"2017-01-17T15:00:46Z","last_update_date":"2019-01-10T15:09:28Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Library"],"version":["1.10.0"],"collections":["BioConductor"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:mirsynergy","credit":[{"email":"yueli@cs.toronto.edu","name":"Yue Li","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["User manual"],"url":"http://bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/html/Mirsynergy.html"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Transcriptional regulatory element prediction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0438"}]}],"name":"Mirsynergy","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"bioconductor_import","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btu373","metadata":{"abstract":"\u00a9 2014 The Author.Motivation: Identification of microRNA regulatory modules (MiRMs) will aid deciphering aberrant transcriptional regulatory network in cancer but is computationally challenging. Existing methods are stochastic or require a fixed number of regulatory modules.Results: We propose Mirsynergy, an efficient deterministic overlapping clustering algorithm adapted from a recently developed framework. Mirsynergy operates in two stages: it first forms MiRMs based on co-occurring microRNA (miRNA) targets and then expands each MiRM by greedily including (excluding) mRNAs into (from) the MiRM to maximize the synergy score, which is a function of miRNA-mRNA and gene-gene interactions. Using expression data for ovarian, breast and thyroid cancer from The Cancer Genome Atlas, we compared Mirsynergy with internal controls and existing methods. Mirsynergy-MiRMs exhibit significantly higher functional enrichment and more coherent miRNA-mRNA expression anti-correlation. Based on Kaplan-Meier survival analysis, we proposed several prognostically promising MiRMs and envisioned their utility in cancer research.Availability and implementation: Mirsynergy is implemented/available as an R/Bioconductor package at www.cs.utoronto.ca/\u223cyueli/Mirsynergy.html.","authors":[{"name":"Li Y."},{"name":"Liang C."},{"name":"Luo J."},{"name":"Wong K.-C."},{"name":"Zhang Z."}],"citationCount":58,"date":"2014-09-15T00:00:00Z","journal":"Bioinformatics","title":"Mirsynergy: Detecting synergistic miRNA regulatory modules by overlapping neighbourhood expansion"},"pmid":"24894504","type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Functional, regulatory and non-coding RNA","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0659"},{"term":"Gene transcripts","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3512"},{"term":"RNA","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0099"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"Mirsynergy","home":"https://bio.tools/mirsynergy","license":"GPL-2.0","version":"1.10.0","summary":"Detect synergistic miRNA regulatory modules by overlapping neighbourhood expansion.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:mirsynergy:1.10.0/cmd/bioconductor.org","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"sourcecode":{"interpreted":true}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"documentation":{"manual":true},"license":{"open_source":true,"osi":true},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":32,"citations":[{"count":1,"year":"2014"},{"count":1,"year":"2017"},{"count":1,"year":"2022"},{"count":1,"year":"2024"},{"count":1,"year":"2026"},{"count":2,"year":"2021"},{"count":2,"year":"2023"},{"count":2,"year":"2025"},{"count":4,"year":"2015"},{"count":5,"year":"2018"},{"count":5,"year":"2019"},{"count":7,"year":"2016"}],"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/bt